WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.940
Animals take in oxygen and remove carbon dioxide by diffusion across large, thin,

2
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.400
moist surfaces. Gases move from higher partial pressure to lower.

3
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.480
More area, a steeper pressure difference and a thinner barrier all raise the rate.

4
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.200
That is why the exchange surface matters.

5
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.760
Breathing and blood flow keep the pressure difference up.

6
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:26.793
This is Fick's law.

7
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:30.773
Partial pressure is one gas's share of the total pressure.

8
00:00:31.813 --> 00:00:34.833
Gases dissolve in a water film before crossing cells.

9
00:00:35.513 --> 00:00:36.933
So the surface must stay moist.

10
00:00:37.967 --> 00:00:40.127
Breathing renews the gas at that surface.

11
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:43.667
In mammals, the diaphragm flattens and the ribs lift.

12
00:00:44.527 --> 00:00:47.447
The chest enlarges, pressure falls, and air flows in.

13
00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:52.047
A frog instead raises its mouth floor to push air into the lungs.

14
00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:55.347
Mammal air flows in and out through the same airways.

15
00:00:56.227 --> 00:00:57.407
This is tidal ventilation.

16
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.540
Each breath only partly renews the gas in the alveoli.

17
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.100
The windpipe and bronchi are dead space, where no exchange happens.

18
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:12.220
Alveolar ventilation is breathing frequency times the volume of each breath after

19
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.520
subtracting dead space.

20
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.560
Equal total air movement can mean different amounts of fresh air reaching the

21
00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:19.560
alveoli.

22
00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:22.963
Animals use different surfaces for this shared job.

23
00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:26.023
Earthworms exchange gases through moist skin.

24
00:01:27.143 --> 00:01:30.663
Insects have branching air tubes called tracheae that reach their tissues.

25
00:01:31.743 --> 00:01:35.303
Water holds far less oxygen than air and takes more work to move.

26
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:39.847
A trout's gills have filaments carrying thin plates called lamellae.

27
00:01:40.647 --> 00:01:43.667
Inside each lamella, blood runs opposite to the water.

28
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:48.647
This countercurrent exchange keeps a partial-pressure difference along its whole

29
00:01:48.647 --> 00:01:54.027
length. Blood leaving can approach the oxygen partial pressure of incoming water.

30
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.280
Bird lungs exchange gas in tubes called parabronchi.

31
00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:00.380
Air sacs act as bellows.

32
00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.040
Through most parabronchi, air flows the same way during breathing in and out.

33
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.420
Capillaries cross the airstream.

34
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:09.520
This is crosscurrent exchange.

35
00:02:10.583 --> 00:02:11.783
What controls breathing?

36
00:02:12.723 --> 00:02:16.143
In humans at rest, carbon dioxide sets most of the drive.

37
00:02:17.023 --> 00:02:19.483
It enters the brain and lowers the fluid's pH.

38
00:02:20.863 --> 00:02:22.703
Central chemoreceptors detect that change.

39
00:02:23.503 --> 00:02:28.383
The carotid bodies respond mainly to low arterial oxygen, and also to carbon

40
00:02:28.383 --> 00:02:34.763
dioxide. With carbon dioxide production unchanged, less alveolar ventilation lets

41
00:02:34.763 --> 00:02:37.103
alveolar carbon dioxide pressure rise.

42
00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:41.993
One-way airflow also occurs in alligators and some lizards.

43
00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:45.073
Alligators have no air sacs.

44
00:02:45.213 --> 00:02:47.653
So air sacs are not required for one-way flow.
